    {
      "id": "9653bb54-1b62-43ae-9cb4-65d9430c881d",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Arrange character classes/interfaces exercise",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "9653bb54-1b62-43ae-9cb4-65d9430c881d"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "95b21886-cef3-4c7b-81eb-4fe4d596e66e",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "![Classes and interfaces](image.png)\nYour task:\n\n1. Arrange the classes.\n\n2. Identify one abstract class, one interface, and eight classes.\n- Abstract class: Character\n- Interface: WeaponBehavior\n- Classes: Knight, Queen, King, Troll, SwordBehavior, BowAndArrowBehavior, AxeBehavior, KnifeBehavior\n\n3. Draw arrows between classes.\n\na. Draw this kind of arrow for inheritance (“extends”). \nInheritance arrows point from the superclass to the subclass. Character superclass will have arrows pointing to Knight, Queen, King, and Troll subclasses.\n\nb. Draw this kind of arrow for interface (“implements”).\nInterface WeaponBehavior will have arrows pointing to SwordBehavior, BowAndArrowBehavior, AxeBehavior, and KnifeBehavior classes.\n\nc. Draw this kind of arrow for HAS-A.\nSuperclass Character will have a HAS-A relationship with the WeaponBehavior interface, indicating that each character has a weapon behavior.\n\n4. Put the method setWeapon() into the right class.\nsetWeapon() method should be placed in the Character class, as it allows characters to change their weapon behavior dynamically.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "95b21886-cef3-4c7b-81eb-4fe4d596e66e"
      }
    }
  ]
}